Here is a sample tutorial on how to use the XNA game framework. Please note that the methods used here may not be the most efficient or effective way to accomplish the task.

The XNA Framework can be downloaded from the Microsoft website. The framework can be extended using a Visual C#/C++ compiler to create assemblies.

Create a folder on your PC and add the assemblies you want to use. I named my folder "Common" and the assemblies after their names.

In the folder, create a new file called app.xaml as an empty project using the.NET console.

Open app.xaml.cs and add the following code:

This code defines the assembly we want to use for the window.

Create a new project in your PC by clicking "File -> New -> Project" and enter the name "Hello C#". You now have a new project that can be run as a native application or through Visual Studio.

I added an event to the window and checked the checkbox to display a message when the player presses the right mouse button. Here is the code:

// Initializing the application

using (Windows.UI.Xaml.Navigation.KeyNavigator kv = Navigation.Initialize()) {

kv.Navigate(typeof(App))

}

I created a main class that can be used for all game programming in the app.

After creating an event handler for the "Go to" button, I wrote code to display "Hello from C#" in the window when the player clicks the right button.

The main event handler is as follows:

protected override void OnNavigatedTo(System.EventArgs e)

{

if (!kv.Navigation.HasNextKey)

return;

Application.Screen->Show("Hello C#", kv.Text);

}

If your code is correct and to the point, the "Hello C#" message might appear in the window instead of an image.

