A crucial aspect of implementing a class that maintains a state is handling updates to that state in response to arbitrary events. In the previous post, I discussed the concept of extending an external entity, which can modify its data at any time, just like the state can change. In this post, I will demonstrate this concept in more detail and utilize EntityFramework to create the service implementation.

public class CounterService extends ApiService<CounterService> implements SubCommand { private Context context; // Add a task to execute before each update private Task executeTaskBeforeUpdate(Context context, CounterService.Counter newValue) { // Create the context to store the current value, // and initialize the counters to the value that we will be updating and // set the counters to null for the current state as it has already been set to the previous state // Add the CounterService in the context and configure the tasks to start once they are started, // add an Execute task to run when a condition is met and a Finish task to run when the task is complete. // Set up the methods to return a value. this.context = context; this.counter = new Counter(); context.task.add(Execute.valueOf(newValue)); context.task.add(Finish.valueOf(newValue)); this.counter = null; return (this.context || Context.DISABLED)? null : (this.context!== Context.DEBUG); } }

In place of implementing EntityFramework in your own code, the EntityFramework provides a helper for all these operations. For the case of a CounterService, we just call the EntityFramework.addImplementation helper (that utilizes the existing context and addSynchronizationMethod ) to receive the following implementation in the implementation class (using the existing context ):

public class CounterEntityFramework extends EntityFramework<CounterService> { //... // Add the Context instance, that holds the previous state and updated state entities private Context context = new Context(); // Add the Context instances (all the entities in the code) to EntityFramework public class Command implements SubCommand { #... // Call the implementation class, from ApiContext public CfgExecutableExecutor executor(Context context, Context ctx) { Execute.valueOf(ctx.context.counter.value); Execute.finish(); return executor(context, ctx); } public void execute(Context context, Counter counterValue) { assertEquals(counterValue, context.counter.value, "should be equal before and after"); assertEquals(ctx.entityClassName + " (" + context.counter.value + ")", context.counter.getName()); } public void finish() { ctx.context.counter.set(""); } } #... }

There is no need to call any other specific implementations using this helper as our previous example will just work for any type of entity that implements the EntityInterface.

To test our new implementation of the counter service, we can simply create a new instance of CounterService using the context:

val counter = new Counter("Hello");

